course: french
lesson: 25
title: Le, la, un, une: every word is a boy or a girl
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Every French noun belongs to one of two groups, masculine (le, un) or feminine (la, une), even tables, cars and clouds; it is a habit of the word, not a sign that the thing is male or female.
explain: So a table is la table, a book is le livre, and a man is still une personne, while a baby girl is still un bébé.
explain: Picture a class making two big posters, the le wall and the la wall: le soleil, le gâteau, le fromage go on one; la lune, la voiture, la question on the other, each with a little drawing.
explain: Endings are good clues: -tion, -ure and -ette are usually la, while -age, -eau and -ment are usually le, but every pattern has a few rebels such as la plage and l'eau.
explain: Before a vowel or a silent h, le and la both shrink to l', so l'eau and l'arbre hide their gender - you find it with un or une: une eau, un arbre.
fact: Every French noun is masculine (le, un) or feminine (la, une); it is about the word, not about being a boy or a girl.
fact: Une personne is feminine even for a man, and un bébé is masculine even for a baby girl.
fact: Before a vowel or a silent h, le and la become l': l'eau (feminine), l'arbre (masculine).
fact: Words ending in -tion are feminine: la station, la question, la natation.
fact: Most words ending in -age are masculine (le fromage, le garage, le voyage), but la plage, la page, la cage and l'image are feminine.
fact: Le soleil (the sun) is masculine and la lune (the moon) is feminine; in German it is the other way round.
fact: Une voiture (a car) is feminine and un vélo (a bike) is masculine; many words ending in -ure are feminine, like la confiture (jam) and la peinture (paint).
fact: Most words ending in -eau are masculine (le bateau, le gâteau, le château), but l'eau and la peau (skin) are feminine.
fact: Some animals have a male and a female word: le chien and la chienne, le lion and la lionne; at school the main word, le chien, is enough.
fact: A few words change meaning with gender: un livre is a book and une livre is a pound; un tour is a trip and une tour is a tower, like la tour Eiffel.
fact: Endings -ette, -tion, -ure and -ence are often feminine (la baguette, la chaussette); -ment, -eau, -age and -isme are often masculine (le moment, le bâtiment).
fact: The best habit is to learn every noun with le or la in front, like a surname.
exercise: Amelia asks why a table is "a girl". What is the real reason? | The gender belongs to the word itself | French people think tables look like girls | Tables were first made by women | All furniture in French is feminine
exercise: Which is right for a book? | le livre | la livre | les livre | l'livre
exercise: Jake calls his male teacher "le personne". What is right? | la personne, because the word is feminine | le personne, because the teacher is a man | les personne, because it is polite | l'personne, because it starts with p
exercise: Leo writes "le eau". What is right? | l'eau | la eau | le'eau | les eau
exercise: Why can't you hear the gender of l'arbre? | le shrinks to l' before a vowel | Trees have no gender in French | The word is plural | The a is silent
exercise: Sophie sees a new word ending in -tion. What is her best guess? | la, because -tion words are feminine | le, because -tion words are masculine | l', because -tion words start with a vowel | les, because -tion words are plural
exercise: Which -age word is feminine? | la plage | le fromage | le garage | le voyage
exercise: Ruby says "le soleil et le lune". What should she say? | le soleil et la lune | la soleil et le lune | la soleil et la lune | les soleil et les lune
exercise: Mia says "un voiture rouge". What is right? | une voiture rouge | un voiture rouge | une voiture rougé | la voiture un rouge
exercise: Which group of -eau words is all masculine? | bateau, gâteau, château | bateau, eau, château, gâteau | peau, gâteau, bateau | eau, peau, château, bateau
exercise: Isla asks if a dog is le or la. What is the main school word? | le chien | la chien | la chienne only | le chienne
exercise: Charlie finds "une livre" in the dictionary. What is it? | A pound | A book | A library | A free gift
exercise: What is la tour Eiffel? | The Eiffel Tower | The Eiffel trip | The Eiffel turn | The Eiffel tour bus
exercise: Which ending is usually masculine? | -ment, as in le moment | -ette, as in la baguette | -tion, as in la station | -ure, as in la voiture
exercise: What is the best way to learn a new noun? | With its le or la, like a surname | Without any little word at all | By guessing from what the thing is | By always using le to be safe
test: Your mate from Leeds goes, "Easy, mate: boys' things are le, girls' things are la." What do you tell him? | Gender is about the word: une personne is la even for a man | He's right, a football is le because boys play it | He's right, a car is la because girls drive | Nobody knows, French people just guess
test: ★★ Theo says "-ette means girl and -ment means boy, proper job." Using the clue, what is la chaussette? | A sock, a feminine word ending in -ette | A boy's shoe, a masculine word | A girl named Chaussette | A building, a masculine word ending in -ment
test: Ruby from Belfast says, "Sun's a fella, moon's a girl, so every language does it like that, so it is." Is she right? | No, German does it the other way round | Yes, every language with gender agrees | Yes, because the sun is stronger than the moon | No, French makes the sun feminine too
test: ★★ Harry has learned "-age is le". His teacher writes a sentence about the beach. Which should he write? | la plage, a famous exception | le plage, following the rule | l'plage, because of the vowel | les plage, because beaches are big
test: ★★ A new word on a sign: bâtiment. Without a dictionary, which is most likely? | le bâtiment, with the masculine ending -ment | la bâtiment, with the feminine ending -ment | la bâtimente, adding an e for a building | les bâtiment, because buildings are plural
test: What would happen if you asked a French shop for "un livre de pommes"? | They'd hear "a book of apples", not "a pound of apples" | They'd give you half a kilo of apples | They'd give you a bag of apples for free | They'd show you the way to the library
test: ★★★ Ollie's sister lists five -eau words: bateau, gâteau, eau, château, peau. How many are feminine? | Two: eau and peau | One: only eau | None: all -eau words are masculine | Three: eau, peau and château
test: Mia from Ohio goes, "Okay, it's giving - so my bike's a girl too, une vélo." (it's giving = it seems like) Fix it. | un vélo: bike is masculine, but une voiture | une vélo: bikes are feminine like cars | la vélo: you must use la for bikes | une vélot: you add a t for feminine
test: ★★★ Which list is ALL feminine? | la question, la confiture, la baguette, la page | la question, le moment, la baguette, la page | la plage, le château, la voiture, l'eau | la peau, la cage, le garage, la lune
test: Your cousin in Aberdeen says, "Ach, I'll just say le for everything, nae bother." What's the risk? | He'd often be wrong, and some words change meaning | No risk, French people always use le | No risk, la is only for people's names | He'd be right, because le is the polite form
test: ★★ In a cartoon, a knight says "Je vais à la tour" and rides to a tall castle building. Is that right? | Yes, une tour is a tower | No, it should be le tour for a tower | No, la tour means a bike trip | Yes, la tour means a horse ride
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises grammatical gender in French? | A property of each noun, independent of the sex of its referent | A property determined by the sex of the referent | A property applied exclusively to persons and animals | A property governed by the size of the referent
exam: Identify the article appropriate to the noun "personne" where the person referred to is male. | la | le | l' | les
exam: State which of the following nouns constitutes an exception to the general masculine gender of nouns ending in -age. | plage | fromage | garage | voyage
exam: Ascertain the meaning of "livre" when preceded by the feminine article une. | A pound | A book | A library | A letter
